 the number of maximal chains in A by c(A). We\nwill show that for each ﬁxed α > 0 we have\nmax{c(A) : |A| = α2n} = (α + o(1)) n!\nFor smaller set systems we are unable to answer the question. We conjecture\nthat a ‘tower of cubes’ construction (deﬁned later) is extremal. We ﬁnish by\nmentioning brieﬂy a connection to an extremal problem on posets and a variant\nof our question for the grid graph.\nThis question does not appear to have been asked before, although there has\nbeen work on the problem of maximising the number of k-element chains for\nsmaller k. For k = 2 (that is, maximising the number of comparable pairs) Alon\nand Frankl [1] proved that a tower of cubes is approximately extremal. They\nalso proved a similar but less exact result for arbitrary k (with k ﬁxed and n\nlarge). By contrast, in this note we are interested in the case when k is as large\nas it can be.\nWe note brieﬂy that the question of minimising the number of k-element\nchains has also been studied. In this direction Kleitman [5] proved the following\nquantitative extension of Sperner’s theorem: the minimum number of compara-\nble pairs is achieved by a union of middle layers of P(X). He also conjectured\nthat the same construction minimises the number of k element chains for any k\n– this problem remains open. See also the recent work of Dove, Griggs, Kang\nand Sereni [3].\n∗School of Mathematical Sciences, Queen Mary University of London, London E1 4NS\n†Department of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics, Centre for Mathematical\nSciences, Wilberforce Road, Cambridge CB3 0WB\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSet Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains | Combinatorics, Probability and Computing | Cambridge Core (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/combinatorics-probability-and-computing/article/abs/set-systems-containing-many-maximal-chains/0EDCB8751C0CDDD76CFA09DBFB8B637B)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.1 years ago; Crawled: yesterday; # Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains ... For smaller set systems we conjecture that a ‘tower of cubes’ construction is extremal. ... (1986) Combinatorics, Cambridge University Press.Google Scholar ... Sperner's theorem and a problem of Erdős, Katona and Kleitman.\n\n# Set Systems Containing Many Maximal Chains\n\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  09 October 2014\n\nJ. ROBERT JOHNSON ,\n\nIMRE LEADER  and\n\nPAUL A. RUSSELL\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe purpose of this short problem paper is to raise the following extremal question on set systems: Which set systems of a given size maximise the number of (n + 1)-element chains in the power set Image$\\mathcal{P}$(1,2,. . .,n)? We will show that for each fixed α > 0 there is a family of α2 n sets containing (α + o(1))n! such chains, and that this is asymptotically best possible. For smaller set systems we conjecture that a ‘tower of cubes’ construction is extremal. We finish by mentioning briefly a connection to an extremal problem on posets and a variant of our question for the grid graph.\n\n## Keywords\n\nPrimary 05D05\n\n* * *\n\n## Information\n\nType\n    Problem Papers\n\nInformation\n    \n\nCombinatorics, Probability and Computing , Volume 24 , Issue 3 , May 2015 , pp. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlgebra univers. 56 (2007) 337–347 (https://euclid.colorado.edu/~monkd/monk77.pdf)\nciteturn9search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; # Towers and maximal chains in Boolean algebras ... ** A tower in a Boolean algebra A is a strictly increasing sequence, of regular length, of elements of A with supremum 1. ... length_spec(A) = { |C| : C is a maximal chain in A }. ... We follow Koppelberg [6] for Boolean algebraic notation.\nAlgebra univers. 56 (2007) 337–347  \n0002-5240/07/030337 – 11, published online February 21, 2007  \nDOI 10.1007/s00012-007-2002-8  \n©Birkhäuser Verlag, Basel, 2007  \n\nAlgebra Universalis\n\n# Towers and maximal chains in Boolean algebras\n\nJ. Donald Monk\n\n**Abstract.** A tower in a Boolean algebra A is a strictly increasing sequence, of regular length, of elements of A with supremum 1. We consider the following functions:  \nt_spec(A) = { |T| : T is a tower in A };  \nlength_spec(A) = { |C| : C is a maximal chain in A }.  \n\nThese are called the spectrum of towers of A and the spectrum of maximal chains of A, respectively. Our main results are (1) for any nonempty set K of regular cardinals there is an atomless Boolean algebra A such that t_spec(A) = K; (2) under GCH, an analogous result holds for maximal chains. Note that towers do not exist in some Boolean algebras, for example in the finite-cofinite algebra on an uncountable set.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nOur set-theoretical notation is standard, with some possible exceptions, as follows. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMath. Log. Quart. 58, No. 3, 159 – 167 (2012) / DOI 10.1002/malq.201110064 (https://euclid.colorado.edu/~monkd/monk83.pdf)\nciteturn9search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Maximal chains in Boolean algebras were investigated in [7], but the following simple connection with the\nwell-known tower number was not mentioned.\nProposition 2.6 If A is an atomless Boolean algebra, then t(A) ≤l(A). ... Let C be a maximal chain in A.\nMath. Log. Quart. 58, No. 3 (2012) / www.mlq-journal.org\n161\n|A|\ns\nl\nc\nt\ni\nf\nu\nsmm\na\nπχinf\nr\np\nω\nFig. 1\nAtomless Boolean algebras.\nExample 2.4 provides the simplest known example of an atomless Boolean algebra A such that u(A) <\ni(A). The ﬁrst such example was, consistently, A = P(ω)/ﬁn. In [4], a ZFC example was given with a rather\ncomplicated proof.\nIn connection with f and u the following problem should be mentioned.\nProblem 2.5 Is f(A) = u(A) for atomless Boolean algebras A?\nMaximal chains in Boolean algebras were investigated in [7], but the following simple connectio